WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.040
There is a messed up set of incentives

00:00:02.040 --> 00:00:05.400
that's baked into the
K-12 public school system,

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:06.320
where they get your money

00:00:06.320 --> 00:00:08.660
regardless of whether they
open their doors for business.

00:00:08.660 --> 00:00:11.050
So teachers unions had an incentive

00:00:11.050 --> 00:00:12.620
to keep their doors closed.

00:00:12.620 --> 00:00:13.640
Corey DeAngelis,

00:00:13.640 --> 00:00:16.180
the Reason Foundation's
director of school choice,

00:00:16.180 --> 00:00:18.870
has been tracking the
impact of the pandemic

00:00:18.870 --> 00:00:20.290
on education policy.

00:00:20.290 --> 00:00:22.420
He's found that a
growing number of parents

00:00:22.420 --> 00:00:24.290
are leaving traditional public schools

00:00:24.290 --> 00:00:27.610
and looking to take their
education dollars with them.

00:00:27.610 --> 00:00:28.780
At the same time,

00:00:28.780 --> 00:00:31.320
there's growing frustration
with teachers unions

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:34.230
for their continued
opposition to reopening,

00:00:34.230 --> 00:00:36.640
despite mounting evidence that schools

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:39.060
aren't a significant source of infection.

00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:42.130
Why was it okay for the
private schools to reopen

00:00:42.130 --> 00:00:43.400
but not the public schools?

00:00:43.400 --> 00:00:45.880
The public schools in
some places were saying

00:00:45.880 --> 00:00:48.070
that we can't open for
in-person instruction

00:00:48.070 --> 00:00:49.800
because it's too dangerous.

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:51.880
But then they were reopening the same

00:00:51.880 --> 00:00:54.230
physical school buildings

00:00:54.230 --> 00:00:56.980
for in-person childcare activities.

00:00:56.980 --> 00:00:58.580
DeAngelis says that unions have made

00:00:58.580 --> 00:01:01.120
a host of ridiculous demands as conditions

00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:02.530
for returning to work.

00:01:02.530 --> 00:01:05.700
But they started lumping in
all of these political demands

00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:07.330
in their calls to reopen schools.

00:01:07.330 --> 00:01:09.230
So for example, one of
the first teachers unions

00:01:09.230 --> 00:01:11.890
to do this was the Los
Angeles teachers union.

00:01:11.890 --> 00:01:13.430
They wanted to defund the police,

00:01:13.430 --> 00:01:14.780
they wanted Medicare for all,

00:01:14.780 --> 00:01:17.030
and all these other political
goals that didn't seem

00:01:17.030 --> 00:01:19.800
like they had a lot to do
with the reopening of schools.

00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:21.270
With public schools struggling

00:01:21.270 --> 00:01:23.690
to provide quality online education,

00:01:23.690 --> 00:01:25.930
many parents attempted
to transfer their kids

00:01:25.930 --> 00:01:27.720
into virtual charter schools,

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:29.810
taking their education dollars with them.

00:01:29.810 --> 00:01:33.070
DeAngelis says teachers
unions turn to lawmakers

00:01:33.070 --> 00:01:34.340
to stop the outflow.

00:01:34.340 --> 00:01:36.600
The Oregon Education Association

00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:39.300
lobbied to the government
to make it illegal

00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:42.890
for families to switch to
schools in this time of need.

00:01:42.890 --> 00:01:44.720
And you saw similar actions in places

00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:47.370
like California and Pennsylvania,

00:01:47.370 --> 00:01:49.280
where the money wouldn't follow the child

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:50.350
to the charter schools.

00:01:50.350 --> 00:01:52.710
This was an act that
protected the monopoly

00:01:52.710 --> 00:01:55.050
at the expense of thousands of families.

00:01:55.050 --> 00:01:56.950
As a result of slow and incompetent

00:01:56.950 --> 00:01:58.670
rollouts of distance learning,

00:01:58.670 --> 00:02:00.660
students around the country are failing

00:02:00.660 --> 00:02:02.210
at much higher rates.

00:02:02.210 --> 00:02:04.040
So for example, in my area,

00:02:04.040 --> 00:02:06.140
Fairfax County public schools,

00:02:06.140 --> 00:02:07.940
the rates of students
who have been failing

00:02:07.940 --> 00:02:10.740
two or more classes has increased by 83%

00:02:10.740 --> 00:02:12.030
since last year.

00:02:12.030 --> 00:02:13.570
There's been a nationwide study

00:02:13.570 --> 00:02:15.540
on this by McKinsey and Company,

00:02:15.540 --> 00:02:17.540
and they've estimated
that students have lost

00:02:17.540 --> 00:02:21.990
between one and three months
of education this year,

00:02:21.990 --> 00:02:25.350
and they've seen that there
have been inequities as well.

00:02:25.350 --> 00:02:27.760
For disadvantaged groups of students,

00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:29.930
they've lost more months of learning

00:02:29.930 --> 00:02:32.000
than more advantaged groups of students.

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:33.250
The silver lining is that

00:02:33.250 --> 00:02:34.670
there's growing recognition

00:02:34.670 --> 00:02:37.860
that traditional education
funding is deeply flawed,

00:02:37.860 --> 00:02:40.330
and that money needs to follow the child.

00:02:40.330 --> 00:02:42.260
The latest national survey from Gallup

00:02:42.260 --> 00:02:45.090
estimates that traditional
public schools will lose

00:02:45.090 --> 00:02:48.730
about seven percentage points
of their student population

00:02:48.730 --> 00:02:49.750
this school year.

00:02:49.750 --> 00:02:51.870
More and more people
are starting to realize

00:02:51.870 --> 00:02:53.980
these closed buildings are continuing

00:02:53.980 --> 00:02:56.581
to get their children's education dollars.

00:02:56.581 --> 00:02:57.970
while they're scrambling,

00:02:57.970 --> 00:02:59.400
and none of that money's following them

00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:01.830
to pay for private school
tuition and fees out of pocket,

00:03:01.830 --> 00:03:03.630
or to help cover the costs

00:03:03.630 --> 00:03:06.670
associated with a home-based education.

00:03:06.670 --> 00:03:10.300
86% of families now support the concept

00:03:10.300 --> 00:03:11.920
of education savings accounts,

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:14.380
or what I like to call "funding students

00:03:14.380 --> 00:03:15.860
as opposed to systems."

00:03:15.860 --> 00:03:18.730
I've been tracking about 14 states now,

00:03:18.730 --> 00:03:22.360
this year, in January,
that have had legislators

00:03:22.360 --> 00:03:26.570
introduce bills to fund
students as opposed to systems.

00:03:26.570 --> 00:03:28.470
So states like New Hampshire,

00:03:28.470 --> 00:03:31.000
Utah has a particular
bill that's interesting.

00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:33.000
Kansas and Kentucky

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:37.604
Arizona has a bill to expand
their already existing

00:03:37.604 --> 00:03:38.874
school choice program.

00:03:38.874 --> 00:03:41.274
You have Nebraska, you have Iowa.

00:03:41.274 --> 00:03:44.824
Florida has a bill to restructure
all of their vouchers.

00:03:44.824 --> 00:03:46.654
A lot of people are figuring out

00:03:46.654 --> 00:03:49.634
that there's no good
reason to fund institutions

00:03:49.634 --> 00:03:52.277
when we can fund students
directly instead.

